{
  "requirement": "Before that commit, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to check that the book holds exactly the agreed text.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. Before the commit, the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to check that the book holds exactly the agreed text.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_checks_the_book_holds_exactly_the_agreed_text]` calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`. That helper reads the bundled skill through `bundled_skill`, which joins `SKILL.md` with its reference files and collapses whitespace. `assert_text` then asserts that the text contains the phrase \"Check with `git diff` that the book holds exactly the agreed text, then commit it\".\n\nThe real `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` step 5 (Commit) contains that sentence, wrapped across lines. The whitespace collapse makes the match work.\n\nThe phrase covers both parts of the obligation:\n- It directs the agent to check that the book holds exactly the agreed text.\n- It puts the check before the commit, through \"then commit it\".\n\nIf the directive were removed or reworded, the assertion would fail with a panic. I found no gap, so I judge the test adequate. A single phrase assertion is the right form here, because the requirement is about the skill's text, and it has no other modes or boundary cases.",
